WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.744
An AI agent opens a supplier document.

2
00:00:04.402 --> 00:00:06.310
It should review delivery terms.

3
00:00:06.677 --> 00:00:08.886
But that same document can tell it to

4
00:00:08.906 --> 00:00:12.091
reveal confidential data or change its
task.

5
00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:16.016
The next tool call is where an instruction
becomes an incident.

6
00:00:16.673 --> 00:00:19.313
ActionGate checks that action before it
runs.

7
00:00:19.506 --> 00:00:23.179
It connects agents to models, APIs and MCP
services

8
00:00:23.326 --> 00:00:26.337
through a gateway that enforces the
organization's rules.

9
00:00:26.995 --> 00:00:30.737
Here is a real supplier review using
synthetic business data.

10
00:00:31.395 --> 00:00:34.196
We start the interactive checks in the
dashboard.

11
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.819
The agent reads its approved document
through MCP,

12
00:00:37.931 --> 00:00:40.489
asks the local business model for a
summary,

13
00:00:40.601 --> 00:00:43.763
saves protected memory and creates an
internal report.

14
00:00:44.316 --> 00:00:46.584
Open the document operation and you can
see

15
00:00:46.604 --> 00:00:50.067
the actual connector receipt alongside the
decision.

16
00:00:50.528 --> 00:00:52.784
Now compare three other outcomes.

17
00:00:53.244 --> 00:00:56.162
Personal data is redacted before the
report is saved.

18
00:00:56.414 --> 00:00:58.542
An instruction that tries to redirect

19
00:00:58.562 --> 00:01:01.213
the workflow is blocked before dispatch.

20
00:01:01.581 --> 00:01:05.996
And a request for another tenant's
document is rejected by access policy.

21
00:01:06.457 --> 00:01:11.337
That access boundary remains in force
independently of the semantic model.

22
00:01:11.588 --> 00:01:13.821
The same controls follow the workflow.

23
00:01:14.282 --> 00:01:17.432
Reading confidential information restricts
later actions,

24
00:01:17.545 --> 00:01:19.963
including delegated work and memory.

25
00:01:20.621 --> 00:01:23.074
Publishing a protected report requires
approval of

26
00:01:23.094 --> 00:01:26.105
its exact content and permitted recipient.

27
00:01:26.902 --> 00:01:29.576
Operators manage a signed control catalog.

28
00:01:29.886 --> 00:01:32.885
Change the profile, adjust a threshold or
publish

29
00:01:32.905 --> 00:01:36.403
a threat rule, then inspect the active
generation.

30
00:01:36.957 --> 00:01:38.888
Strict can block personal data.

31
00:01:39.256 --> 00:01:40.873
Balanced can redact it.

32
00:01:41.427 --> 00:01:43.671
Policy comparison shows how a candidate

33
00:01:43.691 --> 00:01:47.085
changes decisions without repeating
business effects.

34
00:01:47.742 --> 00:01:50.092
Budgets are checked before execution.

35
00:01:50.552 --> 00:01:53.400
The dashboard separates token usage, local

36
00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.465
worker occupancy and commercial API
commitments.

37
00:01:57.123 --> 00:01:59.774
Delegating work shares the original
allowance.

38
00:02:00.328 --> 00:02:03.315
Security teams can trace an operation
through dispatch and

39
00:02:03.335 --> 00:02:08.807
settlement, while management sees resource
totals without protected payloads.

40
00:02:09.058 --> 00:02:11.767
Developers can connect through HTTP, a

41
00:02:11.787 --> 00:02:14.763
Chat Completions interface or MCP.

42
00:02:15.223 --> 00:02:18.744
Python and TypeScript examples, executable
tests and a

43
00:02:18.764 --> 00:02:22.530
runbook make the system available for
independent evaluation.

44
00:02:22.677 --> 00:02:23.644
Change a rule.

45
00:02:23.896 --> 00:02:25.223
Try your own input.

46
00:02:25.591 --> 00:02:27.127
Follow the resulting action.

47
00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.716
ActionGate gives teams the controls and
evidence

48
00:02:29.736 --> 00:02:31.771
to put useful agents to work.
